# Examples from the `ggplot2` documentation {#sec-ggplot}

The following examples are adapted from the
[ggplot2 book's coord chapter](https://ggplot2-book.org/coord.html#polar-coordinates-with-coord_polar).

::: {.callout-caution}
All of the examples on this page require the `coord-polar` branch of
[iangow/plotnine](https://github.com/iangow/plotnine/tree/coord-polar).
The standard version of plotnine does not include the `coord_polar()` and `coord_radial()` functions used here.

The source code for this repository can be found [here](https://github.com/iangow/coord-polar-test) and includes a `pyproject.toml` file that can be used with `uv` to install everything needed to run all the examples here.
:::

::: {#29a26c58 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import numpy as np
import polars as pl
from plotnine import (
    ggplot, aes,
    geom_bar, geom_path, geom_point,
    coord_polar,
    scale_x_discrete, scale_y_continuous,
    theme,
)
from plotnine.data import mtcars
```
:::


## Bar → Pie → Bullseye (mtcars)

A stacked bar chart becomes a pie chart when `theta = "y"` maps height (count)
to angle, and a bullseye chart when the default `theta = "x"` maps the discrete
x position to angle instead.

::: {#b158abd3 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
mtcars_pl = (
    pl.from_pandas(mtcars)
    .with_columns(
        pl.lit("1").alias("x"),
        pl.col("cyl").cast(pl.Utf8),
    )
)
```
:::


::: {#cell-fig-mtcars-bar .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(
    ggplot(mtcars_pl, aes(x="x", fill="cyl"))
    + geom_bar(width=1)
    + scale_x_discrete(expand=(0, 0))
    + scale_y_continuous(expand=(0, 0))
    + theme(legend_position="none", figure_size=(4, 4))
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
![Stacked bar chart](ggplot2-examples_files/figure-html/fig-mtcars-bar-output-1.png){#fig-mtcars-bar width=384 height=384}
:::
:::


::: {#cell-fig-mtcars-pie .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(
    ggplot(mtcars_pl, aes(x="x", fill="cyl"))
    + geom_bar(width=1)
    + coord_polar(theta="y")
    + scale_x_discrete(expand=(0, 0))
    + scale_y_continuous(expand=(0, 0))
    + theme(legend_position="none", figure_size=(4, 4))
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}
![Pie chart: coord_polar(theta='y')](ggplot2-examples_files/figure-html/fig-mtcars-pie-output-1.png){#fig-mtcars-pie width=384 height=384}
:::
:::


::: {#cell-fig-mtcars-bullseye .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
(
    ggplot(mtcars_pl, aes(x="x", fill="cyl"))
    + geom_bar(width=1)
    + coord_polar()
    + scale_x_discrete(expand=(0, 0))
    + scale_y_continuous(expand=(0, 0))
    + theme(legend_position="none", figure_size=(4, 4))
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}
![Bullseye chart: coord_polar()](ggplot2-examples_files/figure-html/fig-mtcars-bullseye-output-1.png){#fig-mtcars-bullseye width=384 height=384}
:::
:::


## Polar path (spiral)

In ggplot2 a straight line in Cartesian space becomes a spiral in polar
coordinates. `theta = "x"` maps the x-column (here: angle in radians) to the
angular axis; y maps to radius.

::: {#cell-fig-polar-spiral .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
n = 100
spiral = pl.DataFrame({
    "r":     np.linspace(0, 1, n),
    "theta": np.linspace(0, 3 / 2 * np.pi, n),
})

(
    ggplot(spiral, aes(x="theta", y="r"))
    + geom_path()
    + geom_point(size=2, color="red")
    + coord_polar()
    + theme(figure_size=(5, 5))
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=6}
![A Cartesian line becomes a spiral under coord_polar()](ggplot2-examples_files/figure-html/fig-polar-spiral-output-1.png){#fig-polar-spiral width=480 height=480}
:::
